1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,115
We are now in the
magnificent Hall of Mirrors,
one of the most evocative
places on the entire tourist
route through the Grotta del
Bue Marino. In this section,
the water is usually

2
00:00:13,115 --> 00:00:24,991
so still and flat that
it becomes a perfect
reflecting surface. The
walls and formations mirror
themselves, creating
an upside-down image
of extraordinary clarity,

3
00:00:24,991 --> 00:00:37,231
giving the illusion of
a depth far greater than
it actually is. It is,
however, only an optical
illusion: at the deepest
point accessible to the
public, the water never

4
00:00:37,231 --> 00:00:50,710
exceeds two metres. The
formation of these "mirrors"
depends greatly on external
weather conditions. When
a dry wind blows outside,
such as the maestrale, the
air remains stable and the

5
00:00:50,710 --> 00:01:04,189
surface of the water stays
perfectly still, allowing
the reflections to appear
in all their perfection. If,
on the other hand, a humid
wind is blowing outside,
such as the scirocco, the

6
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:16,065
moisture penetrates into the
cave. Here it meets
a naturally cooler
environment and
a condensation effect
occurs: the warm, humid air,
upon contact with the cooler

7
00:01:16,065 --> 00:01:29,544
walls, produces tiny water
droplets. In these cases,
inside the Hall of Mirrors
a kind of very fine drizzle
can begin, which ripples the
surface of the water and
makes the mirror effect

8
00:01:29,544 --> 00:01:42,659
disappear. The temperature
inside the cave is in fact
constant throughout the
year, ranging between 16 and
18 degrees Celsius. The
relative humidity is very
high and can reach 99%.

9
00:01:42,659 --> 00:01:55,118
It is precisely this
delicate balance between
temperature, humidity, and
external conditions that
determines the magic —
or the absence — of the
mirrors. This hall is also

10
00:01:55,118 --> 00:02:07,577
one of the best places
to observe the colours
of the cave. White is the
natural colour of the
limestone, the sedimentary
rock of which the entire
mountain is composed. The

11
00:02:07,577 --> 00:02:20,254
red-orange is due to the
presence of iron oxide,
a common element in karst
environments. The brown
tones are linked to detrital
deposits: mud, soil, and
materials transported

12
00:02:20,254 --> 00:02:32,421
by water over time. Also
visible on the walls is
a thin horizontal brown
line, which runs from the
Hall of Candelabras
to approximately the halfway
point of the route.

13
00:02:32,421 --> 00:02:45,171
On closer inspection,
it turns out to be composed
of oyster fossils dating
back approximately 120,000
to 125,000 years. This line
is evidence of an ancient
sea level, probably

14
00:02:45,171 --> 00:02:58,723
following a glacial period.
With the melting of the ice,
the sea rose to a height
greater than today, allowing
oysters to settle along the
cave walls. With the
subsequent lowering of the

15
00:02:58,723 --> 00:03:12,056
sea level, these organisms
became fossilised, leaving
a trace that is still
perfectly visible today. The
same line marking that
ancient sea level can also
be observed along the coasts

16
00:03:12,056 --> 00:03:24,078
of the Gulf of Orosei, where
it appears as a clear
wave-cut notch carved into
the rock. Here, in the Hall
of Mirrors, we are not
merely observing
reflections: we are

17
00:03:24,078 --> 00:03:28,960
observing time, climate, and
geological history imprinted
in stone.
